[J.Purchase@cs.ucl.ac.uk (Jan Purchase) writes:]
[] While resting in a room (being careful not to typeahead spaces), an incubus
[] appeared right next to him [her?] and took off his gloves! He
[] was instantly turned to stone -- all in one round!

There seem to be two frustrating things contributing to this death:
(1) An incubus/succubus/nymph(?) can remove the player's gloves without
removing the player's weapon, which appears unrealistic.
(2) A cockatrice corpse kills instantly on contact, while the live
cockatrice sometimes takes several turns to kill.

Re (1) above:

If a demon is stone resistant, then it is possible for the demon to
handle a cockatrice corpse.  In general, it might be reasonable for
the demon to disarm the player, which would logically precede glove
removal.  For example, depending on what MPAA rating is desired, a
seducing demon might say (cover your eyes if easily offended):

- Put down your weapon.  You'll be glad you surrendered.
- Put down that corpse.  I want to smell your lovely perfume.
- Put down your mace.  It's scratching my face.
- Put down your katana.  It's scratching my banana.
- Put down your whip.  I'm not into S&M.
- Put down your dagger.  It's your long sword I want.

As for nymphs, I haven't heard that they are stone resistant, so it
would be hard from a nymph to remove gloves from a player who wields a
cockatrice corpse.  Perhaps a nymph could persuade the charmed player to
drop the corpse first.

Re (2) above:

Is the cockatrice's hissing different from direct contact with the
player's exposed skin?  Does a live cockatrice kill naked monsters
instantly with a touch?  How does a cockatrice cause injury when it
hits armor?  Does a player die instantly for hitting a cockatrice with
bare hands?  For the sake of consistency, it seems that touching a
cockatrice corpose ought to be no worse than touching the live beast.

